-- title:
--   Integrality of Y²jmatĥ^{ m}(jmatĥ-1728)^m over ℂ[jmatĥ⁻¹]
-- statement:
--   Fix a positive integer $N$ and a natural number $m$, and let $f$ be a modular form of weight $2m$ for the congruence subgroup $\Gamma_0(N)$. Write $\hat\jmath$ for the Laurent series `coeffEmb ℂ jq` over $\mathbb{C}$, that is, the coefficientwise image under $\mathbb{Q}\to\mathbb{C}$ of `jq` $= q^{-1}\cdot$(the rational power series `jNumQ` obtained from the integral series `jNum` by base change along $\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{Q}$); and write $\theta$ for the $\mathbb{C}$-linear operator `thetaL ℂ` on $\mathbb{C}((q))$ given by $\theta g = q\cdot g'$, the product of the monomial $q$ with the formal derivative of $g$. Let $Y$ be a Laurent series over $\mathbb{C}$ satisfying $Y\cdot(\theta\hat\jmath)^m = \hat f$, where $\hat f$ denotes the $q$-expansion of $f$ of width $1$, viewed as a power series and then as a Laurent series. The conclusion is that the element $$Y^2\,\hat\jmath^{\,m}\,(\hat\jmath - 1728)^m$$ of $\mathbb{C}((q))$ is integral over the $\mathbb{C}$-subalgebra of $\mathbb{C}((q))$ generated by the single element $\hat\jmath^{-1}$ (the inverse being taken in the field of Laurent series), where $1728$ is understood via the structure map $\mathbb{C}\to\mathbb{C}((q))$.
--
--   This is the $q$-expansion form of the statement that $f^2/E_4^{\,m}$, a weight-zero meromorphic modular function for $\Gamma_0(N)$ with poles only above $j=0$, is integral over $\mathbb{C}[1/j]$; the identities $\hat\jmath = \hat E_4^3/\hat\Delta$, $\theta\hat\jmath = -\hat E_4^2\hat E_6/\hat\Delta$ and the structure of level-one forms of weight divisible by $12$ are the inputs cited. It feeds the variant [`ModularCurve.isIntegral_adjoin_coeffEmb_jq_inv_of_coeffMap_mul_thetaL_pow_eq_qExpansion`](thm.html#ModularCurve.isIntegral_adjoin_coeffEmb_jq_inv_of_coeffMap_mul_thetaL_pow_eq_qExpansion), used in the integrality analysis of $q$-expansions on the modular curve $X_0(N)$.
